    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yScs0qto22RG"
      },
      "source": [
        "We got results from SMOTE that are nearly indistinguishable from class\n",
        "weighting: PR-AUC 0.3560 vs 0.3556, F2 0.0805 vs 0.0805, MCC 0.1005 vs\n",
        "0.1005, and a confusion matrix that differs by only 20 transactions out of\n",
        "555,719 (93,610 false positives vs 93,630). Recall is in fact identical\n",
        "between the two — both catch exactly 1,673 of the 2,145 test frauds (0.780)\n",
        "— with precision differing only in the fourth decimal (0.01756 vs 0.01755).\n",
        "We don't think this convergence is coincidental; it has a mechanical\n",
        "explanation. For a linear model like Logistic Regression, oversampling the\n",
        "minority class to parity and reweighting the loss inversely by class\n",
        "frequency are two different implementations of essentially the same idea —\n",
        "both push the fitted decision boundary to treat the two classes as if they\n",
        "occurred with equal frequency, and both leave the model's actual capacity\n",
        "to separate the classes (its ranking ability, reflected in PR-AUC) largely\n",
        "untouched. SMOTE achieves this by duplicating the effective presence of\n",
        "fraud examples in the loss surface through synthetic interpolation; class\n",
        "weighting achieves it by scaling the loss contribution of each fraud\n",
        "example directly. On a six-feature linear decision boundary, these\n",
        "converge to nearly the same solution. We take this as a useful negative\n",
        "result: generating 1.28 million synthetic points added meaningful\n",
        "computational cost without buying any accuracy the much cheaper\n",
        "class-weighting approach didn't already provide."
      ],
      "id": "yScs0qto22RG"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "SA2XwzHu22RG"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Focal Loss"
      ],
      "id": "SA2XwzHu22RG"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "w_-a4e2V22RG"
      },
      "source": [
        "Focal loss has no scikit-learn parameter, so we had to make a real\n",
        "design decision before writing code. We implement it here as a small\n",
        "custom logistic regression (a single linear layer plus sigmoid, trained\n",
        "with PyTorch) rather than a gradient-boosted model with a custom objective,\n",
        "for a specific reason: Control, Class Weights, and SMOTE all use the exact\n",
        "same linear model architecture, so the only variable that changes between\n",
        "them is the imbalance-handling strategy itself. Swapping in a GBM for Focal\n",
        "Loss would confound two changes at once — a different model class and a\n",
        "different loss — and any performance difference wouldn't cleanly attribute\n",
        "credit to the loss function versus the model. Keeping the architecture\n",
        "identical to a plain logistic regression isolates the loss function as the\n",
        "sole variable, matching how we compared the other three strategies."
      ],
      "id": "w_-a4e2V22RG"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 10,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "FMGhbXw222RH",
        "outputId": "6d8afc6a-5b46-4449-d21d-7b87dc004308"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "Epoch 0: loss=0.1359\n",
            "Epoch 40: loss=0.0452\n",
            "Epoch 80: loss=0.0259\n",
            "Epoch 120: loss=0.0170\n",
            "Epoch 160: loss=0.0123\n",
            "PR-AUC: 0.3482\n",
            "F2:     0.0000\n",
            "MCC:    0.0000\n",
            "Predicted positives: 0 out of 555719\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "import torch\n",
        "import torch.nn as nn\n",
        "\n",
        "# 1. Same train/test tensors, same scaled features every other strategy used\n",
        "X_train_t = torch.tensor(X_train_scaled, dtype=torch.float32)\n",
        "y_train_t = torch.tensor(y_train.values, dtype=torch.float32).unsqueeze(1)\n",
        "X_test_t = torch.tensor(X_test_scaled, dtype=torch.float32)\n",
        "\n",
        "# 2. Same architecture as plain Logistic Regression - one linear layer + sigmoid -\n",
        "#    so this comparison isolates the loss function, not the model itself\n",
        "torch.manual_seed(42)\n",
        "model_focal = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(X_train_t.shape[1], 1), nn.Sigmoid())\n",
        "\n",
        "# 3. Focal loss - down-weights easy, already-well-classified examples so the rare,\n",
        "#    harder-to-classify fraud cases dominate the gradient more than they would under\n",
        "#    plain log-loss. alpha=0.25, gamma=2.0 are the standard values from Lin et al.\n",
        "#    (2017, RetinaNet) - not tuned here, just the literature default.\n",
        "def focal_loss(y_pred, y_true, alpha=0.25, gamma=2.0, eps=1e-7):\n",
        "    y_pred = torch.clamp(y_pred, eps, 1 - eps)\n",
        "    p_t = y_true * y_pred + (1 - y_true) * (1 - y_pred)\n",
        "    alpha_t = y_true * alpha + (1 - y_true) * (1 - alpha)\n",
        "    loss = -alpha_t * (1 - p_t) ** gamma * torch.log(p_t)\n",
        "    return loss.mean()\n",
        "\n",
        "# 4. Plain Adam training loop, full-batch - only 6 features, this is cheap even at 1.3M rows\n",
        "optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model_focal.parameters(), lr=0.01)\n",
        "\n",
        "for epoch in range(200):\n",
        "    optimizer.zero_grad()\n",
        "    y_pred = model_focal(X_train_t)\n",
        "    loss = focal_loss(y_pred, y_train_t)\n",
        "    loss.backward()\n",
        "    optimizer.step()\n",
        "    if epoch % 40 == 0:\n",
        "        print(f\"Epoch {epoch}: loss={loss.item():.4f}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# 5. Score on the same untouched test set, same three metrics, same 0.5 threshold\n",
        "with torch.no_grad():\n",
        "    proba_focal = model_focal(X_test_t).numpy().ravel()\n",
        "pred_focal = (proba_focal >= 0.5).astype(int)\n",
        "\n",
        "pr_auc_focal_default = average_precision_score(y_test, proba_focal)\n",
        "f2_focal_default = fbeta_score(y_test, pred_focal, beta=2)\n",
        "mcc_focal_default = matthews_corrcoef(y_test, pred_focal)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"PR-AUC: {pr_auc_focal_default:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"F2:     {f2_focal_default:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"MCC:    {mcc_focal_default:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(\"Predicted positives:\", int(pred_focal.sum()), \"out of\", len(pred_focal))\n"
      ],
      "id": "FMGhbXw222RH"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "gJ_L9mvG22RH"
      },
      "source": [
        "The loss curve converges cleanly (0.1359 to 0.0123 over 200 epochs,\n",
        "monotonically decreasing), so training itself is working. But F2 and MCC\n",
        "land at exactly 0.0000, meaning zero predicted positives crossed the 0.5\n",
        "threshold. We traced this to how `alpha_t` is defined: `alpha_t =\n",
        "y_true*alpha + (1-y_true)*(1-alpha)`. With `alpha=0.25`, fraud cases (`y=1`)\n",
        "get weight 0.25 and legitimate cases (`y=0`) get weight 0.75 — the\n",
        "already-rare fraud class is further down-weighted relative to the majority\n",
        "class, not boosted. This is a correct reading of Lin et al.'s (2017) own\n",
        "convention: `alpha=0.25` was tuned for RetinaNet's foreground/background\n",
        "object detection, where the focusing term `(1-p_t)^gamma` does the real\n",
        "imbalance-correcting work and alpha is a secondary, empirically-chosen\n",
        "fine-tune specific to that problem. Applied unmodified to our 0.579%-fraud\n",
        "tabular problem, that same alpha actively works against what we need here.\n",
        "Rather than assume a better value, we test alpha across a range next, the\n",
        "same way we resolved every other design decision in this project (raw hour\n",
        "vs. `is_night`, log vs. banded amount, log vs. clipped velocity)\n",
        "empirically rather than assuming it."
      ],
      "id": "gJ_L9mvG22RH"
    },
