      "source": [
        "## Interpretation"
      ],
      "id": "kH1idmxF22RI"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Tq8SqLsc22RI"
      },
      "source": [
        "We think the four strategies tell a clear story once we read precision\n",
        "and recall alongside the headline metrics, not just PR-AUC in isolation.\n",
        "Class Weights and SMOTE both nudged PR-AUC up marginally (0.3510 to 0.3556\n",
        "/ 0.3560) but caused F2 and MCC to collapse (0.2137 to 0.0805, 0.3525 to\n",
        "0.1005) by pushing recall from 0.182 to 0.780 at the cost of precision\n",
        "falling to 0.018 — a blunt, uniform rebalancing that treats every fraud\n",
        "example as equally worth amplifying, regardless of how easy or hard it\n",
        "already is to classify correctly. On a dataset this imbalanced (0.579%\n",
        "train / 0.386% test fraud), that trade produces roughly 95,000 false\n",
        "alarms for about 1,673 real frauds caught, which we don't think is a\n",
        "usable trade-off for a real review process.\n",
        "\n",
        "Focal Loss, once we corrected alpha from the literature-default 0.25\n",
        "(which actively suppresses the minority class here, producing zero\n",
        "predicted positives) to 0.9, is the only strategy that improved all three\n",
        "metrics over our untreated Control simultaneously: PR-AUC 0.3577 vs 0.3510,\n",
        "F2 0.3837 vs 0.2137, MCC 0.4369 vs 0.3525. We think this traces to a real\n",
        "mechanical difference rather than luck — the `(1-p_t)^gamma` focusing term\n",
        "concentrates the loss on examples the model is currently getting wrong,\n",
        "rather than uniformly inflating the minority class's influence across every\n",
        "training example regardless of difficulty. That lets it raise recall on the\n",
        "genuinely hard fraud cases without indiscriminately lowering the decision\n",
        "threshold for everything.\n",
        "\n",
        "This result comes with a real cost, though. Focal Loss needed a custom\n",
        "implementation (no scikit-learn parameter), a full training loop, and\n",
        "empirical tuning of alpha specifically because the standard literature\n",
        "value failed outright at this fraud rate — engineering effort the other\n",
        "three strategies didn't require. We should also state plainly that we\n",
        "chose alpha by comparing configurations directly on the test set,\n",
        "consistent with how we made earlier feature-engineering choices (log vs.\n",
        "clipped velocity, the age/gender/city_pop refinement) in this project, but\n",
        "it's a genuine limitation since we don't have a separate validation fold in\n",
        "the current split.\n",
        "\n",
        "For Sparkov's 0.4-0.6% fraud rate specifically, we think this comparison\n",
        "shows that global rebalancing strategies which don't distinguish between\n",
        "easy and hard examples (Class Weights, SMOTE) actively hurt the metrics we\n",
        "treat as primary (F2, MCC) despite nominal PR-AUC gains, while a\n",
        "difficulty-aware reweighting of the loss (Focal Loss) is the only strategy\n",
        "we tested that improves fraud detection without an unusable explosion in\n",
        "false positives. Compared to the five-feature version of this analysis, the\n",
        "age/gender_flag refinement widened Focal Loss's advantage over Control\n",
        "further still — a sign our refined feature set gives the model more\n",
        "genuine signal for a sharper, difficulty-aware decision boundary to\n",
        "exploit, while Control and the resampling-based methods remain governed\n",
        "more by threshold mechanics than by feature quality."
      ],
