      "source": [
        "## Model Robustness Check — Random Forest\n",
        "\n",
        "We used the same Logistic Regression architecture across all four\n",
        "imbalance strategies above, deliberately kept constant so the\n",
        "imbalance-handling strategy was the only variable we were testing. That\n",
        "leaves open a real question we want to check: do our conclusions hold for a\n",
        "model that can capture non-linear feature interactions, or are they an\n",
        "artifact of using a linear decision boundary? In this section we repeat\n",
        "Control, Class Weights, and SMOTE using a Random Forest instead, on the\n",
        "same six-feature set and the same untouched test fold, to check whether our\n",
        "earlier findings generalize across model types."
      ],
      "id": "hSBIXmX-22RI"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 13,
      "metadata": {
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/"
        },
        "id": "co_PTUuC22RI",
        "outputId": "f7d7f2a9-fc0a-41ab-ff92-2a6c9418b88c"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "stream",
          "name": "stdout",
          "text": [
            "PR-AUC: 0.8146\n",
            "F2:     0.7389\n",
            "MCC:    0.7761\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# Random Forest doesn't need scaled features - tree splits are based on value\n",
        "# ordering, not magnitude - so we reuse the original unscaled X_train / X_test\n",
        "# from the Control cell above instead of the scaled versions we needed for\n",
        "# Logistic Regression and Focal Loss.\n",
        "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "model_rf_control = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=42, n_jobs=-1)\n",
        "model_rf_control.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "proba_rf_control = model_rf_control.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n",
        "pred_rf_control = (proba_rf_control >= 0.5).astype(int)\n",
        "\n",
        "pr_auc_rf_control = average_precision_score(y_test, proba_rf_control)\n",
        "f2_rf_control = fbeta_score(y_test, pred_rf_control, beta=2)\n",
        "mcc_rf_control = matthews_corrcoef(y_test, pred_rf_control)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"PR-AUC: {pr_auc_rf_control:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"F2:     {f2_rf_control:.4f}\")\n",
        "print(f\"MCC:    {mcc_rf_control:.4f}\")\n"
      ],
      "id": "co_PTUuC22RI"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yQ8qRiZq22RJ"
      },
      "source": [
        "Random Forest's untreated Control result gave us a dramatic jump over\n",
        "Logistic Regression's Control on the identical six-feature set and test\n",
        "fold: PR-AUC more than doubles (0.3510 to 0.8146) and F2/MCC roughly triple\n",
        "(0.2137 to 0.7389, 0.3525 to 0.7761) — all with no imbalance handling\n",
        "applied at all. This already exceeds every Logistic Regression variant we\n",
        "tested earlier, including our best-performing Focal Loss configuration\n",
        "(PR-AUC 0.3577, F2 0.3837, MCC 0.4369). We think the likely mechanism is\n",
        "that Random Forest can carve non-linear, interaction-aware decision\n",
        "boundaries — for example, a specific combination of category, hour, and\n",
        "velocity together — rather than a single global weighted sum of features,\n",
        "which appears far better suited to how fraud patterns are structured in\n",
        "this feature space than a linear model's single hyperplane."
      ],
      "id": "yQ8qRiZq22RJ"
    },
