    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_3motvcD22RJ"
      },
      "source": [
        "We found `class_weight=\"balanced\"` changed almost nothing for Random\n",
        "Forest. Precision moved from 0.8418 to 0.8370 and recall from 0.7170 to\n",
        "0.7180 — a shift of only 13 predicted positives out of 555,719 (1,827 to\n",
        "1,840). We find this a striking contrast with Logistic Regression, where\n",
        "the same setting increased predicted positives by two orders of magnitude\n",
        "(570 to 95,303) and collapsed precision by a factor of roughly 39. We trace\n",
        "the difference to what class weighting actually reweights: for Logistic\n",
        "Regression, it rescales the loss driving a single global linear boundary,\n",
        "so reweighting shifts that one boundary dramatically. Random Forest instead\n",
        "grows many trees that recursively partition the feature space into small,\n",
        "local regions — by default with no depth limit — so each tree can already\n",
        "isolate minority-class examples through its structure rather than through a\n",
        "globally reweighted loss. Class weighting only nudges which splits get\n",
        "chosen at the margins here; there is no single global threshold for it to\n",
        "blow up. We think this is consistent with a broader, well-documented\n",
        "property of tree ensembles: they are inherently less sensitive to class\n",
        "imbalance than linear or other parametric models, precisely because they\n",
        "do not rely on one global decision boundary that a skewed class\n",
        "distribution can drag off course."
      ],
      "id": "_3motvcD22RJ"
    },
